throughout history it's been rare for a  country to request to be annexed  it's even rarer for the answer to that  question to be no  but when the mongolian people's republic  asked the ussr several times to be  incorporated into it the answer was  always that  which raises the obvious question why  why did the ussr refuse to annex  mongolia  first it should be noted just how an  independent mongolia came to be  and the ussr's role in that as many of  you will know mongolia was a part of  china under the qing dynasty  that was until at all to use a technical  term went to hell and mongolia declared  its independence in 1911.  it was briefly reincorporated into the  new republic of china in 1919 before  declaring its independence again two  years later  at this point the russian civil war was  well underway and it soon spilled over  into mongolia and it was occupied by  communist troops  now the new ussr did agree with the  chinese that mongolia was still a  rightful part of china  after a series of talks in 1924 the  soviets agreed to withdraw  but the timetable for this would be set  at another meeting except for one issue  the ussr was now under new mustachioed  leadership who simply opted to postpone  the meeting to monday the 4th of never  and then created a communist government  in mongolia  from its inception communist mongolia  was a soviet puppet state  however unlike its neighbor to the north  the tuvan people's republic mongolia  wasn't annexed into the ussr  and the primary reason for this was to  avoid war both with china and also japan  from 1952 onwards mongolia was ruled by  a man called yum jaglin zeddenbol  look i'm not even gonna pretend that  pronunciation was any good sedenbaal  over the course of his premiership  asked to be incorporated into the ussr  at least six times  and each and every time the answer was  no so why  well it was down to the fact that the  ussr could get everything it wanted from  mongolia without any sort of negatives  that would come from annexing it  also when you look at all of the states  that the ussr did annex they were by and  large all former parts of the russian  empire and annexing states from outside  of these borders wasn't something any  soviet leaders were interested in  largely because the ussr wanted to  cultivate an international reputation of  being anti-imperialist and pinching  neighbours didn't fit with that  mongolia's economy had largely been  built by the ussr to serve the needs of  moscow  and mongolia was incorporated into the  soviet economy nearly to the same extent  as say kazakhstan or uzbekistan  it's just that having it as a nominally  independent nation would spare the upset  of the ussr's valuable chinese allies to  the south  and also it was a card that the ussr  always wanted to hold in case it needed  diplomatic leverage with the chinese  theoretically the status of mongolia  could always be settled at a later date  and always in china's favor if the right  situation ever arose  which is why despite mongolia's asking  the ussr always refused to annex it  mongolia was already giving it  everything that it wanted  i hope you enjoyed this episode and  thank you for watching with a special  thanks to my patrons  james bissonette kelly moneymaker danny  maloney marvin cassell the pastry  section rob waterhouse  mo aaron the white james castaneda  jordan longley gustav swann marcus  asner jerry lamdin rasheed ali cooling  castleman spilling three plates david  silverman maggie paxkowski coppertone  izzy spencer lightfoot phobojo matthew  shipley  robert wetzel lexy schwinn and winston k  wood
 

